\section{The [[LearnlogRepo]] class}

The class needs four operations: running Git commands, initialising the
repository, beginning a run (committing the code state and the run
header), and finalising a run (committing the results).
We introduce the class shell here and fill in each method in its own
section below.

One run spans both of the last two operations, so the instance also
carries the identity of the run currently in progress: the run id that
links its two commits together, and the command it records.
[[finalize_run]] needs both, and an instance handles exactly one run
because each captured process creates its own [[LearnlogRepo]].

<<classes>>=
class LearnlogRepo:
    """Manages a ``.learnlog/`` Git repository.

    Uses ``--git-dir=.learnlog --work-tree=.`` so the student's
    working directory is the work tree.  The ``.learnlog/`` directory
    contains only Git internals.
    """

    def __init__(self, workdir):
        self.workdir = pathlib.Path(workdir)
        self.git_dir = self.workdir / GIT_DIR_NAME
        self.env = {
            **os.environ,
            "GIT_DIR": str(self.git_dir),
            "GIT_WORK_TREE": str(self.workdir),
        }
        self.run_id = None
        self.run_script = None
        self.run_args = []
        self.init_repo()

    <<repo methods>>
@

The constructor used to call [[init_repo]] only when [[.learnlog/]] was
missing.
Deciding from the directory alone is what a concurrent first run breaks
(\cref{sec:init-repo}): the directory appears the instant one process
starts initialising, so a second process constructed in that window
would conclude that a repository was ready and run its first
[[git add]] against one that was not there yet.
[[init_repo]] is idempotent and answers that question properly, so the
constructor simply always asks it.

The directory name appears in three places that must agree: the path we
hand to Git, the exclude pattern that keeps the log out of its own
history (\cref{sec:init-repo}), and the diagnostics file we write inside
it (\cref{sec:diagnostics}).
A constant makes that agreement checkable instead of a coincidence of
three string literals.

<<constants>>=
GIT_DIR_NAME = ".learnlog"
@


\section{Running Git commands}

Every Git command needs the same environment variables and working
directory.
The [[git]] method wraps this boilerplate and returns the
[[CompletedProcess]] object for callers that need to inspect the result.

Beyond that boilerplate, three decisions in this one method determine
whether a run is recorded at all: what happens when Git fails, how long
we are willing to wait for it, and how the commit message reaches Git.
Each of them has cost us runs, so we take them in turn before assembling
the method.
A fourth decision---whether Git's output comes back as text or as
bytes---costs no runs, but it decides who can use the wrapper at all,
and a caller who cannot use it opts out of the other three as well.

\subsection{Failure is an exception, not a return code}

The wrapper used to hand every [[CompletedProcess]] back unexamined,
which made \enquote{Git refused} indistinguishable from \enquote{Git
succeeded} unless the caller remembered to look.
Almost none did.
Since the calls that matter most---[[add]], [[commit]]---are the ones
whose result nobody inspected, a failing Git simply erased the run:
[[.learnlog/index.lock]] held by a concurrent run, a scrubbed [[PATH]]
under [[env -i]], a full disk.
Nothing was written and nothing was said.

Raising is the right default because the failure modes are not local to
one call site: whoever ends up handling the exception can report it
(\cref{sec:diagnostics}), whereas an ignored return code cannot be
recovered further up.
Callers that genuinely want the return code---[[git diff]] with
[[--cached --quiet]], where the exit status \emph{is} the answer to
\enquote{did anything change?}---opt out with [[check=False]] and get
the old behaviour.

The opt-out covers only the exit status.
A missing [[git]] binary or an expired timeout still raises even under
[[check=False]], because there is no exit status to interpret in those
cases: treating them as \enquote{Git said no} would let
[[check=False]] callers read [[returncode]] off a process that never
ran.

<<classes>>=
class GitError(RuntimeError):
    """A Git command run by learnlog failed.

    Carries the command, its exit code (``None`` when Git could not
    be run at all) and whatever Git wrote to standard error, so the
    diagnostics path can report something actionable.
    """

    def __init__(self, args, returncode, stderr):
        self.command = ("git",) + tuple(args)
        self.returncode = returncode
        if isinstance(stderr, bytes):
            stderr = stderr.decode("utf-8", errors="replace")
        self.stderr = (stderr or "").strip()
        <<build the [[GitError]] message>>
        super().__init__(message)
@

Standard error arrives as [[str]] from an ordinary call and as [[bytes]]
from one that asked for a byte stream (\cref{sec:git-bytes}), so the
constructor decodes it.
A diagnostic is text whatever the command it describes was reading: it
ends up in an exception message, on a terminal, and in the errors log
(\cref{sec:diagnostics}).
Decoding with [[errors="replace"]] rather than strictly is what keeps a
failure reportable even when Git's complaint is not valid UTF-8---a
locale mismatch or a mangled file name in the message must not turn a
Git failure into a [[UnicodeDecodeError]] from the error path itself.

We name the attribute [[command]] rather than [[args]] because
[[BaseException.args]] already means something else---the constructor
arguments---and [[super().__init__]] would overwrite ours.

The rendered command is truncated.
Arguments are supposed to be short now that message text goes on
standard input, but this string is appended to a file on disk
(\cref{sec:diagnostics}), and a diagnostics channel that can be made to
write a megabyte per failure is a new problem rather than a fix for the
old one.
The head of the command is what identifies it, so that is what we keep.

<<constants>>=
MAX_REPORTED_COMMAND_CHARS = 200
<<build the [[GitError]] message>>=
status = (
    "could not be run"
    if returncode is None
    else f"exited with status {returncode}"
)
detail = f": {self.stderr}" if self.stderr else ""
command = " ".join(self.command)
if len(command) > MAX_REPORTED_COMMAND_CHARS:
    command = command[:MAX_REPORTED_COMMAND_CHARS] + "..."
message = f"{command} {status}{detail}"
@

\subsection{How long we are willing to wait}

The original 10-second timeout was chosen to bound how long learnlog
may stall a student's program.
It also, unintentionally, bounded how much work Git was allowed to do:
[[git add .]] and [[git commit]] hash the entire work tree, so a
project with a data directory, a build tree, or a virtual environment
that the exclude patterns do not cover can exceed ten seconds on the
first run---and the penalty for exceeding it was not a slow run but a
lost one.

So we separate the two concerns the single timeout was conflating.
Commands that read a ref or a single commit finish in milliseconds; a
minute for them means Git is stuck, not busy.
Commands that walk the whole tree get a budget large enough that only a
genuinely wedged Git (a hung credential helper, an unresponsive network
filesystem) hits it.
Losing a run is worse than delaying one, so both budgets err on the
generous side.

<<constants>>=
GIT_TIMEOUT = 60
GIT_TREE_TIMEOUT = 600
@

\subsection{Message text travels on standard input}
\label{sec:commit-message-stdin}

Linux caps the length of a \emph{single} [[argv]] string at
[[MAX_ARG_STRLEN]], 128\,KiB---a per-string limit, separate from the
much larger limit on the total size of the argument vector, and one
that [[ulimit]] cannot raise.
A commit message is exactly the kind of argument that grows without
bound: the trailer carries the program's whole captured I/O, up to
[[MAX_IOLOG_BYTES]] (\cref{sec:iolog-cap}), which is an order of
magnitude past the cap.
Passing it as [[git commit -m <message>]] therefore made
[[subprocess]] fail with [[OSError: Argument list too long]] before Git
was even started, and a program printing more than about 128\,KB lost
its entire trailer---exit code, I/O and exception alike.

Standard input has no such limit, so [[git commit --file=-]] takes the
message the same way an editor session would.
The two forms are otherwise equivalent: with neither an editor nor an
explicit [[--cleanup]], Git applies the [[whitespace]] cleanup mode in
both cases, so messages are stored exactly as before.

The rule this leaves us with is simple enough to apply without thinking
about lengths at each call site: \emph{message content never travels in
[[argv]]}.
Every commit in this program passes its message through [[input]].

\subsection{Text or bytes}
\label{sec:git-bytes}

Decoding Git's output as text is right for nearly every call we make:
refs, hashes, log output and status are text by construction, and
callers want [[str]].
It is wrong for exactly the calls that hand us a file.
[[git archive]] emits a tar stream, and a tar stream that has been
through a UTF-8 decoder is no longer a tar stream---at best it raises,
at worst it silently becomes replacement characters.

Because the wrapper could not express that, the one caller that needed
it---the ProgSnap2 exporter's code-state extraction
(\cref{sec:extract-code-state})---called [[subprocess.run]] itself, and
in doing so opted out of everything decided above: it repeated the
environment and working directory by hand, ran without a timeout, and
turned a failing [[git archive]] into an empty code state instead of an
error.
That is the general shape of the problem, and the reason the mode
belongs in the wrapper rather than in the caller: a wrapper a caller
cannot use is a wrapper that caller works around, and the workaround
loses \emph{all} the guarantees, not the one it needed to escape.

So the mode becomes a parameter, [[text]], with the same name and
default as [[subprocess.run]]'s.
A sibling [[git_bytes()]] method was the alternative; it would have had
to repeat the failure handling, the timeouts and the standard-input
plumbing, and that repetition is what would drift the next time one of
those three decisions changes.
A parameter cannot drift: there is still one code path, and the mode
only chooses what type comes back out of it.

\subsection{The wrapper}

<<repo methods>>=
def git(self, *args, check=True, timeout=GIT_TIMEOUT,
        input=None, text=True):
    """Run a Git command with the learnlog environment.

    Returns the ``CompletedProcess``.  Raises ``GitError`` when Git
    cannot be run at all (missing binary, timeout) and, unless
    ``check`` is false, when Git exits non-zero.  Text passed as
    ``input`` reaches Git on standard input; commit messages travel
    that way to escape the operating system's argument-size limit.
    With ``text`` false, standard output and standard error come back
    as bytes, for commands whose output is a file rather than a
    message.
    """
    <<run Git, turning process failures into [[GitError]]>>
    if check and result.returncode != 0:
        raise GitError(args, result.returncode, result.stderr)
    return result
@

[[subprocess]] reports \enquote{Git could not be run} in two unrelated
exception hierarchies---[[OSError]] for a missing binary or an
oversized argument list, [[SubprocessError]] for a timeout---so we
catch both and re-raise them as the one type callers have to know
about.

<<run Git, turning process failures into [[GitError]]>>=
try:
    result = subprocess.run(
        ["git"] + list(args),
        cwd=str(self.workdir),
        env=self.env,
        capture_output=True,
        text=text,
        timeout=timeout,
        input=input,
    )
except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError) as error:
    raise GitError(args, None, str(error)) from error
@

Let's verify the helper runs Git commands in the right context, and
that each of the four decisions above holds:

<<test functions>>=
def test_git_runs_command(workdir):
    repo = LearnlogRepo(workdir)
    result = repo.git("status")
    assert result.returncode == 0

def test_git_raises_on_failure(workdir):
    repo = LearnlogRepo(workdir)
    with pytest.raises(GitError):
        repo.git("rev-parse", "--verify", "no/such/ref")

def test_git_error_reports_command_and_stderr(workdir):
    repo = LearnlogRepo(workdir)
    with pytest.raises(GitError) as caught:
        repo.git("cat-file", "-p", "0" * 40)
    assert caught.value.returncode != 0
    assert "cat-file" in str(caught.value)
    assert caught.value.stderr

def test_git_error_truncates_long_command(workdir):
    repo = LearnlogRepo(workdir)
    with pytest.raises(GitError) as caught:
        repo.git("cat-file", "-p", "q" * 5_000)
    head = str(caught.value).split(" exited with")[0]
    assert len(head) == MAX_REPORTED_COMMAND_CHARS + 3

def test_git_check_false_returns_failed_result(workdir):
    repo = LearnlogRepo(workdir)
    result = repo.git(
        "rev-parse", "--verify", "no/such/ref", check=False
    )
    assert result.returncode != 0

def test_git_missing_binary_raises_even_unchecked(workdir):
    """A scrubbed PATH is not a return code to ignore."""
    repo = LearnlogRepo(workdir)
    repo.env["PATH"] = str(workdir / "empty")
    with pytest.raises(GitError) as caught:
        repo.git("status", check=False)
    assert caught.value.returncode is None

def test_git_input_reaches_git(workdir):
    repo = LearnlogRepo(workdir)
    result = repo.git(
        "hash-object", "-w", "-t", "blob", "--stdin",
        input="payload\n",
    )
    blob = result.stdout.strip()
    assert repo.git("cat-file", "-p", blob).stdout \
        == "payload\n"

def test_git_bytes_mode_returns_undecodable_output(workdir):
    """Content Git hands back need not be text at all."""
    repo = LearnlogRepo(workdir)
    content = b"\x89PNG\r\n\x1a\n\x00\xff\xfe"
    (workdir / "logo.png").write_bytes(content)
    blob = repo.git(
        "hash-object", "-w", "logo.png"
    ).stdout.strip()
    result = repo.git("cat-file", "-p", blob, text=False)
    assert result.stdout == content

def test_git_bytes_mode_reports_failures_as_text(workdir):
    """A byte-mode failure is still a readable diagnostic."""
    repo = LearnlogRepo(workdir)
    with pytest.raises(GitError) as caught:
        repo.git("cat-file", "-p", "0" * 40, text=False)
    assert caught.value.returncode != 0
    assert isinstance(caught.value.stderr, str)
    assert caught.value.stderr
    assert "b'" not in str(caught.value)
@


\section{Reporting failures where a human can find them}
\label{sec:diagnostics}

Raising [[GitError]] only moves the problem: the callers in
[[__init__.py]] must keep swallowing everything, because transparency
means a student's program may not crash, and may not acquire extra
output, just because learnlog had a bad day.
What transparency does \emph{not} require is that the failure leave no
trace at all.
Today a dropped run and a clean run look identical from every angle---to
the student, to the teacher reading the log, and to us reading a bug
report---and that is what makes these failures so expensive to diagnose.

So the exception handlers keep swallowing, and [[report_error]] gives
the evidence somewhere to land.
Two channels, because two different people need it at two different
times.
[[.learnlog/errors.log]] is written unconditionally and read later: it
is the record that explains a gap in a student's history weeks after the
fact, and it costs the running program one append.
[[LEARNLOG_DEBUG]] additionally mirrors each entry to standard error,
for whoever is debugging learnlog \emph{now} and should not have to know
that the file exists.

<<constants>>=
ERRORS_LOG_NAME = "errors.log"
DEBUG_ENV_VAR = "LEARNLOG_DEBUG"
<<functions>>=
def report_error(context, error, workdir=None):
    """Record a learnlog failure without disturbing the program.

    Appends a timestamped entry to ``.learnlog/errors.log`` under
    ``workdir`` (the current directory by default) and, when
    ``LEARNLOG_DEBUG`` is set in the environment, mirrors it to the
    real standard error.  Never raises.
    """
    timestamp = datetime.datetime.now().isoformat()
    entry = (
        f"{timestamp} {context}: "
        f"{type(error).__name__}: {error}"
    )
    <<mirror the entry to stderr when debugging>>
    <<append the entry to the errors log>>
@

The mirror writes to [[sys.__stderr__]], not [[sys.stderr]]: by the time
anything can fail, learnlog has usually replaced [[sys.stderr]] with its
own capturing wrapper, and a diagnostic sent through that wrapper would
be recorded in the commit as though the student's program had printed
it.
[[sys.__stderr__]] is [[None]] in interpreters started without a console
(pythonw, some embedded hosts), so we fall back to whatever
[[sys.stderr]] currently is.

<<mirror the entry to stderr when debugging>>=
if os.environ.get(DEBUG_ENV_VAR):
    stream = sys.__stderr__ or sys.stderr
    try:
        print(f"learnlog: {entry}", file=stream)
    except Exception:
        pass
@

We append only to a [[.learnlog/]] that already exists.
The alternative---creating it---would mean that a learnlog failure in a
directory that is not a learnlog project silently starts one, which is
precisely the surprise the diagnostics are supposed to prevent.

Writing the log \emph{inside} the Git directory is what keeps it out of
the history it describes: [[.learnlog]] is the first exclude pattern
(\cref{sec:init-repo}), so [[git add .]] never stages anything under it.
Git's built-in protection does not apply here---that rule is hard-coded
for a directory literally named [[.git]]---which is why the exclude
pattern and this path are derived from the same constant.

Finally, every failure inside [[report_error]] is itself swallowed---
including the path handling, not just the write.
This is the one place where that matters more than it looks: the only
callers are [[except]] blocks whose whole purpose is to stop an
exception from reaching the student's program, so an exception escaping
from here would do precisely the damage those handlers exist to
prevent.
A read-only checkout, a full disk, or a nonsensical [[workdir]] must not
turn a reported failure into an unreported crash.

<<append the entry to the errors log>>=
try:
    git_dir = (
        pathlib.Path(workdir or os.getcwd()) / GIT_DIR_NAME
    )
    if not git_dir.is_dir():
        return
    with open(
        git_dir / ERRORS_LOG_NAME, "a", encoding="utf-8"
    ) as log:
        log.write(entry + "\n")
except Exception:
    pass
@

Let's verify both channels, and the exclusion that keeps the log out of
the history:

