\section{Repository initialisation}
\label{sec:init-repo}

On the very first run, we need to create the Git repository, configure
a committer identity, set up exclusion patterns, and make an initial
commit of all source files.

That first run need not be alone.
Two terminals open in the same project, a parallel [[make]], or a
logged program that starts another logged program all reach this method
within milliseconds of each other in a directory that has no
[[.learnlog/]] yet, and each of them finds no repository to use.
Running [[git init]] twice in \emph{sequence} is harmless---it is
deliberately re-runnable---but running it twice at once is not.
Both processes copy Git's template hooks into the new directory, and
the one that loses on any single file dies with \enquote{cannot copy
\ldots{} File exists}, taking its run with it.
Even where both survive, the initial commit does not: two commits made
with no [[HEAD]] to build on are two \emph{root} commits on the same
branch, and the branch update that lands second leaves the other root,
together with every run recorded on top of it, reachable from nothing.

The single-writer lock of \cref{sec:commit-lock} is the right mechanism
and cannot be used as it stands, because the file it locks lives inside
the very directory whose creation is being raced.
Creating that directory ourselves is what makes it usable: [[mkdir]]
with [[exist_ok]] is safe for any number of simultaneous callers, an
empty [[.learnlog/]] is one [[git init]] is perfectly happy to
initialise, and a lock file inside it is kept out of the log by the
same exclude pattern that keeps the rest of the Git directory out.

Under the lock, the question to ask is not whether the directory
exists---it does, we just made it---but whether the repository already
has a commit.
The loser of the race blocks until the winner's initial commit is in,
finds one, and returns without writing anything.

<<repo methods>>=
def init_repo(self):
    """Initialise a new ``.learnlog/`` Git repository.

    Creates the repository, sets a fallback committer identity
    (only if the student has none configured globally), sets up
    exclusion patterns, and makes an initial commit of all files.

    Returns without doing anything when the repository already
    has a commit, so that concurrent first runs share one initial
    commit instead of making a root commit each.
    """
    if self.has_commit():
        return
    self.git_dir.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
    with self.commit_lock():
        <<initialise unless another process got there first>>
@

The check before the lock is the one that runs on every later
construction, and it is there to keep the common case---an established
repository---from queueing behind whatever else is committing.
The check inside the lock is the one that is authoritative.

<<initialise unless another process got there first>>=
if self.has_commit():
    return
self.git("init")
<<set fallback identity if needed>>

<<set up exclude patterns>>
<<initial commit>>
@

\enquote{Has a commit} is the property that matters, rather than
\enquote{has a Git directory}, because the directory appears at the
very start of initialisation and the repository only becomes usable at
the end of it.
[[git rev-parse --verify]] answers it, and this is the first of the
three places in this module where a non-zero exit is the answer rather
than a problem: [[HEAD]] fails to resolve exactly while the branch is
still unborn.
The filesystem answers for free in the one case where it can---a
directory that is not there cannot hold a commit---but every
[[LearnlogRepo]] built on an existing log does pay for one
[[rev-parse]].
That is the price of the correctness above, and it is a small one next
to what the same process is about to do: a run makes two commits, each
of which walks the work tree.

<<repo methods>>=
def has_commit(self):
    """Return whether the repository exists and has a commit."""
    if not self.git_dir.is_dir():
        return False
    probe = self.git(
        "rev-parse", "--verify", "--quiet", "HEAD",
        check=False,
    )
    return probe.returncode == 0
@

Where [[fcntl]] is missing the lock is a no-op
(\cref{sec:commit-lock}), and check-then-initialise is racy again.
That is the same trade as for the commit lock itself: a platform
without [[flock]] gets the protocol without the serialisation, rather
than no learnlog at all.

The learnlog repository inherits the student's global Git identity and
GPG signing configuration.
If a student has not configured Git at all, commits would fail---so we
set a fallback identity only when no global one exists.
This means commits are signed with the student's own key when available,
which is the right thing for a log of \emph{their} work.

We check whether Git can resolve a user name; if not, we provide a
generic fallback.
This handles lab machines where students have never run
[[git config]].
We also disable GPG signing---these machine-generated commits do not
need cryptographic signatures, and students may not have a GPG key
configured.

This probe is the second of those places: [[git config user.name]]
exits~1 precisely when the setting we are asking about is absent.
It therefore runs with [[check=False]].

<<set fallback identity if needed>>=
name_check = self.git("config", "user.name", check=False)
if name_check.returncode != 0:
    self.git("config", "user.name", "learnlog")
    self.git("config", "user.email", "learnlog@localhost")
self.git("config", "commit.gpgsign", "false")
