Every remaining property above holds within a single process, and the
one this section exists for does not: it needs several processes
reaching an empty directory together.
Separate processes rather than threads, because the lock they contend
on is a kernel object shared between processes, and because that is the
situation the students are in.

Each of them runs the same short program.
It has to be started with [[python -S]], an explicit [[PYTHONPATH]] and
[[LEARNLOG_SKIP_AUTOSTART]], for a reason specific to this test: if
[[import learnlog]] logged its own run, the repository would be created
during the import, before the test had a chance to line the processes
up, and the race under test would never happen.

<<test functions>>=
CONCURRENT_INIT_SOURCE = """\
import datetime
import pathlib
import sys
import time

from learnlog.gitrepo import LearnlogRepo

workdir, script, deadline = sys.argv[1], sys.argv[2], sys.argv[3]
time.sleep(max(0.0, float(deadline) - time.time()))

repo = LearnlogRepo(pathlib.Path(workdir))
repo.begin_run(script, [], datetime.datetime(2026, 1, 1, 10, 0))
print(repo.run_id)
repo.finalize_run(
    io_log="",
    exception_info=None,
    end_time=datetime.datetime(2026, 1, 1, 10, 0, 1),
    exit_code=0,
)
"""
@

Lining them up is the whole difficulty.
Interpreter startup takes far longer, and varies far more, than the
window between \enquote{no repository here} and \enquote{initial commit
written}, so processes merely launched in a loop would arrive one after
another and the test would pass on the broken code.
Each child therefore sleeps until a wall-clock instant the parent
picked in advance, which costs one fixed delay and puts every child at
[[LearnlogRepo]] within a scheduling quantum of the others.
A machine too slow to reach the deadline in time only makes the test
less concurrent, never wrong.

<<test functions>>=
CONCURRENT_INIT_PROCESSES = 4
CONCURRENT_INIT_BARRIER_SECONDS = 0.5
@

Two things are then true of a correctly initialised log, and each of
them fails on its own kind of damage.
There is exactly one root commit reachable from [[HEAD]], which a
second initial commit breaks.
And every run contributes its id twice, once from its header commit and
once from its trailer, which breaks when a branch update lands on top
of a root that another process had already replaced---the commits
survive as objects, but nothing reaches them any more.

<<test functions>>=
def test_concurrent_init_keeps_one_root_and_every_run(
        workdir):
    """Simultaneous first runs share one initial commit."""
    env = os.environ.copy()
    checkout = pathlib.Path(__file__).resolve().parents[2]
    env["PYTHONPATH"] = str(checkout / "src")
    env["LEARNLOG_SKIP_AUTOSTART"] = "1"
    env.pop("LEARNLOG_PROJECT_ROOT", None)
    deadline = time.time() + CONCURRENT_INIT_BARRIER_SECONDS

    processes = [
        subprocess.Popen(
            [
                sys.executable, "-S", "-c",
                CONCURRENT_INIT_SOURCE,
                str(workdir), f"run{n}.py", str(deadline),
            ],
            cwd=str(workdir),
            env=env,
            stdout=subprocess.PIPE,
            stderr=subprocess.PIPE,
            text=True,
        )
        for n in range(CONCURRENT_INIT_PROCESSES)
    ]
    run_ids = []
    for process in processes:
        out, err = process.communicate(timeout=60)
        assert process.returncode == 0, err
        run_ids.append(out.strip())
    assert len(set(run_ids)) == CONCURRENT_INIT_PROCESSES

    repo = LearnlogRepo(workdir)
    roots = repo.git(
        "rev-list", "--max-parents=0", "HEAD",
    ).stdout.split()
    assert len(roots) == 1
    log = repo.git("log", "--format=%B").stdout
    for run_id in run_ids:
        assert log.count(f"Run-Id: {run_id}") == 2
@


\section{One run, two commits}
\label{sec:two-commit-protocol}

A run produces information at two different moments: what the student
was about to run---the command, its arguments, and the code as it
looked right then---and how it went---the end time, the exit code, the
captured I/O, and any exception.
Writing the first half down \emph{before} the program starts is what
makes the log crash-resilient: a program killed by [[SIGKILL]] never
reaches our exit handler, but its header is already committed.

Earlier versions completed such a record with [[git commit --amend]].
That is only correct while learnlog is the sole writer and nobody has
seen the commit yet, and neither assumption holds:

\begin{description}
\item[Overlapping runs] [[--amend]] rewrites whatever sits at
  \emph{HEAD}, not the commit this run created.  A program that starts
  another logged program, a run left in the background, or a
  [[learnlog]] command overlapping a student run all end with a
  different commit at HEAD, so the results are appended to a stranger's
  run block: one run looks like it crashed, the other reports someone
  else's output.
\item[Published history] Amending replaces a commit that
  [[learnlog push]] may already have sent and [[learnlog tag]] may
  already point at.  The next push is rejected as non-fast-forward, and
  the tag is left behind on an abandoned commit.
\item[Timestamps] An amend keeps the original author date, so runs
  folded into one commit all share the first run's timestamp, and the
  interval between runs (\cref{commits}) is wrong for every run but
  that one.
\end{description}

So we never rewrite anything.
[[begin_run]] writes a \emph{header commit} carrying a fresh run id,
and [[finalize_run]] writes a separate \emph{trailer commit} that
repeats that run id and carries the results.
Each process only ever \emph{adds} a commit, and it names the run it is
talking about instead of trusting HEAD, which is what makes the
protocol safe under any interleaving.

Folding consecutive runs of unchanged code into one commit goes away
with the amend that implemented it: every run now gets its own header
commit, even when it records no change at all.
Empty commits cost a few dozen bytes---the tree is shared---and in
exchange a run's record no longer depends on what the previous run
wrote.

The price is that [[learnlog git log]] shows two commits per run.
Every learnlog reader---[[list]], [[play]], and the ProgSnap2
exporter---joins the pair back into a single record by run id
(\cref{commits}), so the two-commit structure is visible only to
someone inspecting the Git history directly.


\subsection{Which code state belongs to a run}

The header commit also decides \emph{when} the code is snapshotted, and
that is a second thing the old protocol got wrong: it staged the work
tree at the end of the run, so a program that wrote [[a.out]] committed
that binary into its own run.

The code state that explains a run is the code that was executed, not
what the execution produced.
We therefore snapshot at the start and leave the tree alone afterwards.
Files written during the run are not lost: the next run's snapshot
picks them up, where they appear as a change that happened between the
two runs---which is exactly what they are.
If the student never runs anything again, those files never enter the
log, and a build artefact is no loss.


\subsection{Run identity}

A run id has to be unique within one repository, and it is minted by
processes that cannot see each other---the whole point is that they do
not coordinate---so a counter is not an option.
A random UUID is: 122 random bits make a collision far less likely than
a disk error.

<<functions>>=
def new_run_id():
    """Return a fresh run identifier.

    The id links a run's header commit to its trailer commit.
    """
    return uuid.uuid4().hex
@


\subsection{Serialising commits}
\label{sec:commit-lock}

Two learnlog processes can reach [[begin_run]] at the same instant.
Git guards its own data structures with lock files, but a losing
command \emph{fails} ([[index.lock]] already exists) rather than
waiting, and a dropped run is data loss.
We would rather wait a few milliseconds, so we take an exclusive
[[flock]] on a file in [[.learnlog/]] around each commit.

The lock covers one commit operation and nothing more.
A lock held from [[begin_run]] to [[finalize_run]] would make two
terminals take turns \emph{running programs} instead of taking turns
\emph{committing}---a student's program would block on another
student's program, which breaks transparency far worse than the race it
prevents.

Repository creation takes this same lock (\cref{sec:init-repo}),
because the initial commit is a commit that must happen exactly once
too.
The lock therefore means \enquote{learnlog is writing to this
repository}, not \enquote{a run is being committed}, and that is why
its file has to live somewhere creation can be relied on to have made:
inside [[.learnlog/]], which [[init_repo]] creates before it takes the
lock.

[[fcntl]] does not exist on Windows.
Rather than fail there, we degrade to no locking: the protocol is still
append-only, and the remaining window is the one Git itself guards.

<<optional locking support>>=
try:
    import fcntl
except ImportError:  # pragma: no cover - Windows has no fcntl
    fcntl = None
@

<<repo methods>>=
@contextlib.contextmanager
def commit_lock(self):
    """Hold an exclusive lock while writing one commit."""
    handle = self._acquire_lock()
    try:
        yield
    finally:
        if handle is not None:
            handle.close()
@

Closing the file releases the lock, which also means the kernel releases
it if the process dies mid-commit---no stale lock can wedge the next
run.
Failing to acquire the lock is not worth aborting a run over, so every
error path here returns [[None]] and the commit proceeds unserialised.

<<repo methods>>=
def _acquire_lock(self):
    """Return a locked file handle, or ``None`` if unavailable."""
    if fcntl is None:
        return None
    try:
        handle = open(self.git_dir / LOCK_FILE_NAME, "w")
        fcntl.flock(handle.fileno(), fcntl.LOCK_EX)
    except OSError:
        return None
    return handle
<<constants>>=
LOCK_FILE_NAME = "learnlog.lock"
@


\subsection{A private index per run}

Staging with [[git add .]] writes the repository's shared index.
Two concurrent runs sharing that file do not merely collide on
[[index.lock]]: the loser may also snapshot the winner's staged state,
so one run's commit would carry another run's tree.
Pointing [[GIT_INDEX_FILE]] at a per-run file makes each snapshot
private, and Git then has nothing left to contend on.

We set the variable on the environment dict that [[git]] passes to every
command rather than threading a parameter through it, so the wrapper
stays a plain Git runner.
A [[LearnlogRepo]] instance serves a single run, so no two commits share
this dict.

The file is removed when the commit is done.
A process killed in the middle leaves its index behind, but the name
contains the run id, so nothing ever reuses it, and Git ignores every
file in its directory that it did not ask for.

<<repo methods>>=
@contextlib.contextmanager
def run_index(self, name):
    """Point Git at a private index file named after the run."""
    index_path = self.git_dir / f"{RUN_INDEX_PREFIX}{name}"
    self.env["GIT_INDEX_FILE"] = str(index_path)
    try:
        yield index_path
    finally:
        self.env.pop("GIT_INDEX_FILE", None)
        try:
            index_path.unlink(missing_ok=True)
        except OSError:
            pass
<<constants>>=
RUN_INDEX_PREFIX = "index-"
@


\section{Beginning a run}
\label{sec:beginning-a-run}

At the start of each program execution, [[begin_run]] snapshots the work
tree and commits it together with the run header.

The change detection is the third and last place in this module where
a non-zero exit is data rather than trouble.
[[git diff --cached --quiet]] answers \enquote{is the index different
from [[HEAD]]?} with its exit status, so it runs with [[check=False]]
while everything around it does not.

<<repo methods>>=
def begin_run(self, script, args, start_time):
    """Record the start of a program run.

    Snapshots the current code state and commits it with the run
    header, which carries the run id that :meth:`finalize_run`
    will refer back to.

    Returns the run header string.
    """
    self.run_id = new_run_id()
    self.run_script = script
    self.run_args = list(args)
    header = format_run_header(
        script, args, start_time, self.run_id,
    )

    with self.commit_lock():
        <<commit the code state with the run header>>

    return header
@

The commit is always made, with [[--allow-empty]] for the common case
where the student re-runs unchanged code.
Its tree is the work tree as it looks now, which is the state the
program is about to execute.

<<commit the code state with the run header>>=
with self.run_index(f"{self.run_id}-begin") as index_path:
    <<seed the private index from the shared one>>
    self.git("add", ".", timeout=GIT_TREE_TIMEOUT)
    self.git(
        "commit", "--allow-empty", "--file=-", input=header,
        timeout=GIT_TREE_TIMEOUT,
    )
    <<publish the private index as the shared one>>
@

An index is also a stat cache: Git re-hashes a file only when its
[[stat]] data differs from what the index recorded.
Starting each run from an empty index would therefore re-read the whole
project on every run, so we seed the private index with a copy of the
shared one, and move it back afterwards.
Moving it back keeps the cache warm for the next run and keeps
[[learnlog git status]] honest---an index left behind at repository
initialisation would report every later commit as unstaged work.

The copy has to carry the index's own modification time with it, and
that is not tidiness: it is what keeps the cache from lying.
Git compares file times at whole-second resolution, so an edit that
lands in the same second as the one the index recorded, and happens not
to change the file's size, is invisible to that comparison.
What saves Git is the \emph{racily clean} rule: an entry whose recorded
time is not older than the index's own modification time may have been
written after the index was, so Git re-reads the file instead of
trusting its [[stat]] data.
Since Git writes the index only after it has stat'ed the file, that
rule covers precisely the window the second-resolution comparison is
blind to.

Copying with a plain [[copyfile]] stamps the new index with the present
moment.
Once that moment falls in a later second than the entry's recorded
time---which it does as soon as a second boundary passes between the
run that wrote the cache and the run that reads it---the rule no longer
fires, and the second-resolution comparison is left to answer alone.
A student who edits a file and re-runs it within one second, which is
the ordinary edit--run rhythm this tool exists to record, then gets a
header commit holding the code from \emph{before} the edit.
This is measured, not feared: in a loop that ran, edited by the same
number of bytes, and ran again, [[copyfile]] recorded the stale version
five times in four hundred sequences, and preserving the timestamp
recorded it none in twelve hundred.
[[shutil.copy2]] copies the modification time along with the contents,
which makes the private index behave like the shared one it stands in
for---all the rule ever assumed.

Seeding remains best-effort, but \enquote{best effort} has to mean
\emph{no cache} rather than \emph{a bad one}.
A copy that fails part-way leaves a truncated file Git cannot read at
all, and one whose timestamp did not survive is the silent-wrong-data
case above.
So a failed seeding removes what it made, and [[git add .]] rebuilds
the cache from nothing: that costs one re-read of the project and
nothing else.

<<seed the private index from the shared one>>=
shared_index = self.git_dir / SHARED_INDEX_NAME
try:
    shutil.copy2(shared_index, index_path)
except OSError:
    index_path.unlink(missing_ok=True)
@

<<publish the private index as the shared one>>=
try:
    os.replace(index_path, shared_index)
except OSError:
    pass
<<constants>>=
SHARED_INDEX_NAME = "index"
@

Let's verify that each run gets its own commit, that the header
carries the run id and the arguments, and that the snapshot is the code
as it was when the run started:

<<test functions>>=
