\subsection{Arguments that fit on one line}
\label{sec:run-arguments}

A line-oriented format asks two things of a value: that it stay on its
line, and that it come back out the way it went in.
Joining the argument list with spaces gives neither.

Two different lists write the same field: one argument reading
[[alpha beta]] and two arguments reading [[alpha]] and [[beta]] both
come out as [[Arguments: alpha beta]], and no reader can tell them
apart afterwards.
For a [[python -c]] run the field stops meaning anything at all,
because there the first argument is the program \emph{source} and the
student's real arguments follow it.

A source may also contain newlines, and then the field is not merely
ambiguous, it is no longer a field.
Measured on the format as it was, a run whose source read
[[import sys]] on one line and [[print("hi")]] on the next gave a
commit subject ending at [[Run: <python -c> import sys]], an
[[Arguments:]] value reading [[import sys]], and a remainder that the
parser (\cref{progsnap2}) scanned as though it were more header
lines---so a source containing a line [[Exit-Code: 99]] was read back
as this run's exit code.

The shell answered this question long ago, and the project already
uses its answer where [[activate]] prints paths into shell code
(\cref{sec:emit-activation}): [[shlex.quote]] turns any string into one
word, and [[shlex.split]] turns the words back into the original list.
Ordinary arguments need no quoting and come through untouched, so
[[Run: hello.py alpha beta]] still reads like the command line the
student typed---which is the whole reason the header is plain text.

<<functions>>=
def format_run_arguments(args):
    """Format an argument list as one header field value.

    Each argument becomes a single shell word, so the list can be
    recovered exactly by ``parse_run_arguments``.  An empty list is
    written as the ``(none)`` marker.
    """
    if not args:
        return NO_ARGUMENTS
    return " ".join(
        shlex.quote(_escape_argument(str(arg))) for arg in args
    )
<<constants>>=
NO_ARGUMENTS = "(none)"
@

What quoting does not do is remove newlines: a quoted string simply
spans lines.
So before quoting we replace the two characters that end a line with a
backslash and a letter, and the backslash itself first, since the
substitution gives it a meaning it did not have before.
Backslash, newline, and carriage return are the entire table: no other
character can break a line, and the one character that could break the
escaping---a NUL---cannot reach us, because the operating system hands
over [[argv]] entries as NUL-terminated strings and a NUL inside one is
therefore not representable.

Deriving the reverse table from the forward one is what keeps the two
in step: an escape added to one is understood by the other without a
second edit.

<<functions>>=
def _escape_argument(text):
    """Return ``text`` with line-ending characters escaped."""
    for character, code in ARGUMENT_ESCAPES:
        text = text.replace(character, "\\" + code)
    return text
<<constants>>=
ARGUMENT_ESCAPES = (
    ("\\", "\\"),
    ("\n", "n"),
    ("\r", "r"),
)
ARGUMENT_UNESCAPES = {
    code: character for character, code in ARGUMENT_ESCAPES
}
@

Reading is the same two steps in reverse.
Both are forgiving, because a header is data we may meet again in a log
written by an older version of this tool or edited by hand: an
unbalanced quote falls back to splitting on whitespace, and a backslash
that escapes nothing we know stays a backslash.
Neither can raise, and the worst either can do is return the words a
naive split would have returned anyway.

<<functions>>=
def parse_run_arguments(value):
    """Return the argument list recorded in an ``Arguments:`` field.

    Inverts ``format_run_arguments``.  A value not quoted that way
    is split on whitespace instead.
    """
    value = value.strip()
    if not value or value == NO_ARGUMENTS:
        return []
    try:
        words = shlex.split(value)
    except ValueError:
        words = value.split()
    return [_unescape_argument(word) for word in words]

def _unescape_argument(text):
    """Return ``text`` with the argument escapes resolved."""
    return ESCAPED_CHARACTER.sub(
        lambda match: ARGUMENT_UNESCAPES.get(
            match.group(1), match.group(0)
        ),
        text,
    )
<<constants>>=
ESCAPED_CHARACTER = re.compile(r"\\(.)")
@

Let's verify that the common case is left alone and that the awkward
one survives:

<<test functions>>=
def test_format_run_arguments_leaves_plain_words_alone():
    """An ordinary command line still reads like one."""
    assert format_run_arguments(["alpha", "beta"]) \
        == "alpha beta"
    assert format_run_arguments([]) == "(none)"

def test_run_arguments_round_trip_plain_words():
    args = ["--verbose", "input.txt"]
    assert parse_run_arguments(
        format_run_arguments(args)
    ) == args

def test_run_arguments_round_trip_source_and_extras():
    """A -c source with spaces and newlines comes back whole."""
    args = ['import sys\nprint("hi there")', "alpha", "beta"]
    value = format_run_arguments(args)
    assert "\n" not in value
    assert parse_run_arguments(value) == args

def test_run_arguments_round_trip_backslashes_and_returns():
    args = ["c:\\path\\n", "a\r\nb", "", "one two"]
    value = format_run_arguments(args)
    assert "\n" not in value and "\r" not in value
    assert parse_run_arguments(value) == args

def test_parse_run_arguments_tolerates_unquotable_text():
    """An old or hand-edited value degrades to a plain split."""
    assert parse_run_arguments("alpha 'beta") == ["alpha", "'beta"]
    assert parse_run_arguments("(none)") == []
    assert parse_run_arguments("") == []
