\section{Finalising a run}
\label{sec:finalising-a-run}

After the program finishes (or crashes), [[finalize_run]] commits the
I/O log, exception information, end time, and exit code as a new
commit that names the run it completes.

If the program was killed by SIGKILL, the [[atexit]] handler never
fires and [[finalize_run]] is never called.
The header commit from [[begin_run]] still stands, so we know a run was
attempted---the missing [[End-Time]] field signals an abnormal
termination, exactly as before.

A trailer written without a preceding [[begin_run]] has no header to
point at.
We still commit it, under a fresh id, rather than throwing the results
away: no reader will show it, but the data is in the repository if
someone goes looking.

That signal is only trustworthy if the \emph{other} ways of losing a
trailer are gone, which is what makes this method the one that most
needed \cref{sec:commit-message-stdin}: it is where the captured I/O
enters the message, so it was the method that failed on
[[MAX_ARG_STRLEN]], and it failed by producing exactly the record a
[[SIGKILL]] leaves behind.

A review run (\cref{sec:review-subcommands}) brings one extra kind of
result with it, which is why the method takes a [[view_trace]].
Its I/O is not worth keeping---the output of [[learnlog play]] is the
log read back, and copying it into the log would nest the history
inside itself---so what a review run has to say is instead \emph{which
commits the student looked at, and how they moved between them}.
The parameter is optional because every other caller has nothing to
put in it, and an ordinary program run then writes exactly the trailer
it wrote before.

<<repo methods>>=
def finalize_run(self, io_log, exception_info,
                 end_time, exit_code, view_trace=None):
    """Complete the current run with a trailer commit.

    Commits the I/O log, exception information, end time, and
    exit code under the run id from :meth:`begin_run`.  Nothing
    already in the repository is modified, and the commit records
    no code change.

    ``view_trace`` is an optional sequence of
    ``(time, commit, action)`` triples naming the commits a
    review run displayed.
    """
    run_id = self.run_id or new_run_id()
    self.run_id = None

    trailer = format_run_trailer(
        io_log, exception_info, end_time, exit_code, view_trace,
    )
    message = format_trailer_commit(
        self.run_script, self.run_args, run_id, trailer,
    )

    with self.commit_lock():
        <<commit the trailer without touching the tree>>
@

Reading HEAD's tree into the private index is what keeps the trailer
free of code changes.
Staging the work tree instead would attribute to the finished run both
the files it wrote and any edit the student made while it was running.
The tree we read is the \emph{current} HEAD's, not the header's: if
another run committed in between, reverting to the header's tree would
undo that run's snapshot.

<<commit the trailer without touching the tree>>=
with self.run_index(f"{run_id}-end") as index_path:
    <<seed the private index from the shared one>>
    self.git(
        "read-tree", "--reset", "HEAD", timeout=GIT_TREE_TIMEOUT,
    )
    self.git(
        "commit", "--allow-empty", "--file=-", input=message,
        timeout=GIT_TREE_TIMEOUT,
    )
@

Reading the tree is not what costs anything here; committing it is.
Every commit begins by refreshing the index, and an index that
[[read-tree]] has just written into an empty file carries no [[stat]]
data at all---each entry's size and timestamps are zeros.
Zeros tell Git nothing about whether the file on disk still matches, so
it falls back on the answer that is always right and re-hashes the
whole work tree.
That is a second full read of the project, on top of the one
[[begin_run]] paid for, and it buys a commit that records no code
change whatsoever.
Measured on a project of five hundred files of 64\,KiB, the trailer
took a median 105\,ms against the header's 8\,ms: the half of the run
that records nothing was the expensive half, by more than a factor of
ten.

So we seed the private index from the shared one exactly as
\cref{sec:beginning-a-run} does---same chunk, same [[copy2]], same
reason---and the entries arrive carrying the [[stat]] data Git recorded
when it last hashed those files.
Seeding alone does not survive a plain [[read-tree]] though: that form
replaces the index outright, and the entries it puts there come from
the tree with their [[stat]] fields zeroed, which is where we started.
The [[strace]] counts confirm it---identical to not seeding at all.

What preserves the cache is the single-tree \emph{merge} form.
For a path whose blob in the index already matches the tree's, Git
keeps the index's entry, [[stat]] data included, and the refresh
becomes one [[lstat]] per file instead of one full read of it.
On the same project the trailer falls to 9\,ms---what the header
costs, which is what a commit that reads nothing new ought to
cost---and the [[openat]] calls of the commit itself drop from 623 to
108.
The saving is the project's bytes, so it shrinks with them: the same
five hundred files at 4\,KiB take the trailer from 16\,ms to 8\,ms.

Seeding means the trailer no longer starts from a blank slate, and the
index it starts from can disagree with [[HEAD]]: a student can stage
files by hand through [[learnlog git add]], and a concurrent run
publishes its own index between our two commits.
The merge is what makes that harmless.
Merging against a \emph{single} tree resolves every disagreement the
same way---the tree wins.
Paths the index has and the tree does not are dropped, paths the tree
has and the index does not are added, and paths both have take the
tree's blob; only the [[stat]] data is borrowed from the index, and
only where the blobs already agree.
What comes out is [[HEAD]]'s tree, entry for entry.

Edits made to the work tree during the run cannot leak in either,
because [[git commit]] commits the index, not the work tree.
The refresh that opens an edited file does so to answer a question
about [[stat]] data, and its only power is to write [[stat]] data back
for entries whose content still matches; an entry whose content has
changed is left exactly as the merge wrote it.
Staging is something only [[git add]] and [[git commit -a]] do, and we
run neither.

That property started as an accuracy requirement---a finished run must
not be credited with code it never executed---but the review runs of
\cref{sec:review-subcommands} now lean on it for their meaning.
A [[learnlog play]] session is long, and it is exactly the time when a
student sits with their history in one window and their editor in the
other.
Because the header stages the work tree at the moment playback starts
and the trailer stages nothing at all, an edit made while reading is
left uncommitted, and the next commit to pick it up is the header of
the next program run.
That is the reading we want: the edit shows up as \enquote{what they
changed after reviewing} rather than as part of the review itself.
Were the trailer ever to stage the work tree, that diff would be
absorbed into the review session and the connection to the run it
belongs to would be lost.

We ask for [[--reset]] rather than [[-m]] because the two differ in one
case, and it is the one we cannot afford.
An index with unmerged entries---what a conflicted [[learnlog pull]]
leaves behind---makes [[-m]] refuse with \enquote{you need to resolve
your current index first}, and since [[git]] raises, a refusal here
would cost the student the trailer, which is the loss the whole
two-commit protocol exists to prevent
(\cref{sec:two-commit-protocol}).
[[--reset]] discards those entries instead, which is precisely what a
command whose job is to reproduce [[HEAD]] should do with them.
Both forms keep the [[stat]] cache.

The index is discarded when the [[with]] block ends, and unlike
[[begin_run]] we do not publish it as the shared one.
The shared index is the student's staging area; the trailer only
borrows it, and handing back a copy with their staged files stripped
out would make [[learnlog git status]] lie about work they had done.

Committing [[HEAD]]'s tree directly with [[git commit-tree]] would
avoid the index and its refresh altogether---285 syscalls against
828---but it also avoids everything [[git commit]] does around the
tree: finding the identity, honouring the repository's configuration,
writing the reflog, moving the branch.
Reimplementing a commit to save one [[lstat]] per file is a poor trade
when the file count is a student project's.

Finally, [[read-tree]] joins the tree-sized commands on
[[GIT_TREE_TIMEOUT]].
It walks the whole tree, so a large project could have spent longer in
it than the message-sized budget allows, and the penalty for that
budget is not a slow run but the lost trailer again.

Two tests hold the seeded index to the promises above.
The first drifts everything that can drift while a run is in
flight---an artefact appears, the source is edited beneath it, a file
is staged by hand---and asks for the trailer's tree to still be
[[HEAD]]'s, entry for entry.
The second conflicts the shared index the way an interrupted
[[learnlog pull]] would and asks for a trailer anyway; it is the test
that fails if [[--reset]] is ever tidied into [[-m]].

<<test functions>>=
def test_finalize_records_head_tree_not_the_index(workdir):
    """The trailer's tree is HEAD's, whatever the index holds."""
    repo = LearnlogRepo(workdir)
    repo.begin_run(
        "hello.py", [], datetime.datetime(2026, 1, 1, 10, 0),
    )
    head_tree = repo.git(
        "rev-parse", "HEAD^{tree}"
    ).stdout.strip()
    (workdir / "a.out").write_text("build artefact\n")
    (workdir / "hello.py").write_text('print("edited")\n')
    (workdir / "staged.txt").write_text("staged by hand\n")
    repo.git("add", "staged.txt")
    repo.finalize_run(
        io_log="",
        exception_info=None,
        end_time=datetime.datetime(2026, 1, 1, 10, 0, 1),
        exit_code=0,
    )
    assert repo.git(
        "rev-parse", "HEAD^{tree}"
    ).stdout.strip() == head_tree
    assert repo.git("show", "HEAD:hello.py").stdout \
        == 'print("hello")\n'

def test_finalize_survives_an_unmerged_index(workdir):
    """A conflicted index must not cost the run its trailer."""
    repo = LearnlogRepo(workdir)
    repo.begin_run(
        "hello.py", [], datetime.datetime(2026, 1, 1, 10, 0),
    )
    head_tree = repo.git(
        "rev-parse", "HEAD^{tree}"
    ).stdout.strip()
    ours = repo.git(
        "hash-object", "-w", "--stdin", input="ours\n",
    ).stdout.strip()
    theirs = repo.git(
        "hash-object", "-w", "--stdin", input="theirs\n",
    ).stdout.strip()
    repo.git(
        "update-index", "--index-info",
        input=f"100644 {ours} 2\tconflict.txt\n"
              f"100644 {theirs} 3\tconflict.txt\n",
    )
    assert repo.git("ls-files", "-u").stdout.strip()
    repo.finalize_run(
        io_log="",
        exception_info=None,
        end_time=datetime.datetime(2026, 1, 1, 10, 0, 1),
        exit_code=0,
    )
    msg = repo.git("log", "-1", "--format=%B").stdout
    assert msg.startswith("Run-End: hello.py")
    assert repo.git(
        "rev-parse", "HEAD^{tree}"
    ).stdout.strip() == head_tree
