\section{Formatting the run trailer}

The trailer contains the end time, exit code, I/O log, and exception
information.
Its text is unchanged from when it was appended to the header inside
one commit message: a reader that joins the two commits produces
exactly the block format that older repositories already contain
(\cref{commits}), so nothing downstream had to learn a second layout.

The view trace of a review run joins that text as [[Viewed:]] lines
directly below [[Exit-Code:]].
Putting them among the fields rather than in a section of their own
is what makes them free: every reader of this format either recognises
a [[Key: value]] line or ignores it, so a log written by this version
stays readable to the parsers that predate it, and the [[--- I/O ---]]
and [[--- exception ---]] markers keep the positions those parsers
search for.
A run with nothing to report writes no lines at all, which is why the
ordinary program run's trailer is byte-for-byte what it always was.

<<functions>>=
def format_run_trailer(io_log, exception_info,
                       end_time, exit_code, view_trace=None):
    """Format the trailer appended by :func:`finalize_run`.

    Contains the end time, exit code, I/O log, and exception
    information.  ``view_trace``, when given, is a sequence of
    ``(time, commit, action)`` triples written out as ``Viewed:``
    lines.
    """
    parts = [
        f"End-Time: {end_time.isoformat()}",
        f"Exit-Code: {exit_code}",
    ]
    parts.extend(
        format_viewed_line(*entry) for entry in view_trace or ()
    )
    parts.extend([
        "",
        "--- I/O ---",
        io_log.rstrip("\n") if io_log else "(no I/O)",
        "",
        "--- exception ---",
    ])
    if exception_info:
        parts.append(exception_info)
    else:
        parts.append("None")
    return "\n".join(parts)
@

Each line answers three questions in the order a reader asks them:
\emph{what} was on screen, \emph{when}, and \emph{how} the student got
there.
The commit therefore comes first even though the recorder
(\cref{sec:review-runs}) timestamps the event first, and the argument
order here follows the recorder so a stored triple can be splatted
straight in.

All three values are bare tokens---a hexadecimal hash, an ISO-8601
timestamp, a single lower-case word---so the quoting apparatus of
\cref{sec:run-arguments} has nothing to do here.
That is a property of what a trace is rather than luck: the hashes come
from Git, and the actions come from a fixed vocabulary the viewer
chooses from, so neither can carry a newline in from outside.
Recording the full hash rather than an abbreviation costs a few
characters and buys an unambiguous join against the log later, when the
history may have grown past the length at which the abbreviation was
unique.

<<functions>>=
def format_viewed_line(viewed_at, commit_hash, action):
    """Format one ``Viewed:`` line of a review run's trailer.

    Arguments come in the order ``learnlog.record_view`` stores
    them; the line itself leads with the commit that was shown.
    """
    return (
        f"{VIEWED_FIELD} {commit_hash} "
        f"{viewed_at.isoformat()} {action}"
    )
<<constants>>=
VIEWED_FIELD = "Viewed:"
@

Let's verify the format:

<<test functions>>=
def test_format_run_trailer_basic():
    end = datetime.datetime(2026, 1, 1, 10, 0, 5)
    trailer = format_run_trailer(
        "stdout: hello\n", None, end, 0
    )
    assert "End-Time: 2026-01-01T10:00:05" in trailer
    assert "Exit-Code: 0" in trailer
    assert "stdout: hello" in trailer
    assert "None" in trailer

def test_format_run_trailer_with_exception():
    end = datetime.datetime(2026, 1, 1, 10, 0, 1)
    trailer = format_run_trailer(
        "", "ValueError: oops", end, 1
    )
    assert "Exit-Code: 1" in trailer
    assert "ValueError: oops" in trailer
@

The view trace has two things to prove, and the second is the one that
protects every existing log: the lines must arrive in the order they
were recorded---a revisit is data, so identical hashes may repeat---and
a run that saw nothing must add nothing.

<<test functions>>=
def test_format_run_trailer_records_the_view_trace():
    """Viewed: lines follow Exit-Code in navigation order."""
    end = datetime.datetime(2026, 1, 1, 10, 0, 5)
    trace = [
        (datetime.datetime(2026, 1, 1, 10, 0, 1), "aaa", "open"),
        (datetime.datetime(2026, 1, 1, 10, 0, 2), "bbb", "next"),
        (datetime.datetime(2026, 1, 1, 10, 0, 3), "aaa", "prev"),
    ]
    trailer = format_run_trailer("", None, end, 0, trace)
    viewed = [
        line for line in trailer.split("\n")
        if line.startswith("Viewed:")
    ]
    assert viewed == [
        "Viewed: aaa 2026-01-01T10:00:01 open",
        "Viewed: bbb 2026-01-01T10:00:02 next",
        "Viewed: aaa 2026-01-01T10:00:03 prev",
    ]
    lines = trailer.split("\n")
    assert lines.index("Exit-Code: 0") < lines.index(viewed[0])
    assert lines.index(viewed[-1]) < lines.index("--- I/O ---")

@pytest.mark.parametrize("trace", [None, []])
def test_format_run_trailer_omits_an_empty_view_trace(trace):
    """A run that viewed nothing writes the trailer it always did."""
    end = datetime.datetime(2026, 1, 1, 10, 0, 5)
    trailer = format_run_trailer("stdout: hi\n", None, end, 0, trace)
    assert "Viewed:" not in trailer
    assert trailer == format_run_trailer(
        "stdout: hi\n", None, end, 0,
    )
@


\subsection{The trailer commit message}

The trailer commit needs a subject line of its own, because Git shows
subjects everywhere ([[git log --oneline]], [[git shortlog]]) and
\enquote{End-Time: \ldots} would read as noise.
We reuse the header's shape with a [[Run-End:]] keyword, so a subject
still says which command the commit is about, and a reader can tell the
two kinds of commit apart by the keyword alone---the same test that
decides which commits are learnlog's own (\cref{sec:filter-learnlog})
can decide which are trailers.

The run id comes first in the body so that the join key sits at a fixed
place, and the trailer text follows verbatim: dropping the subject and
the [[Run-Id:]] line is all a reader needs to do to recover it.

The arguments go through \cref{sec:run-arguments} for the same reason
they do in the header, and here it is the subject line itself that is
at stake: a newline in an argument would make Git's idea of the subject
end in the middle of the command.

<<functions>>=
def format_trailer_commit(script, args, run_id, trailer):
    """Format the commit message for a run's trailer.

    ``script`` and ``args`` repeat the command from the header so
    the commit subject is meaningful on its own; ``run_id``
    identifies the run this trailer completes.
    """
    args_str = format_run_arguments(args)
    return "\n".join([
        f"{TRAILER_PREFIX}{script or '(unknown)'} {args_str}",
        "",
        f"{RUN_ID_FIELD} {run_id}",
        trailer,
    ])
<<constants>>=
TRAILER_PREFIX = "Run-End: "
@

Let's verify that the message carries the join key and that its subject
identifies the command:

<<test functions>>=
def test_format_trailer_commit():
    end = datetime.datetime(2026, 1, 1, 10, 0, 5)
    trailer = format_run_trailer("", None, end, 0)
    message = format_trailer_commit(
        "hello.py", ["arg1"], "abc123", trailer,
    )
    assert message.startswith("Run-End: hello.py arg1")
    assert "Run-Id: abc123" in message
    assert message.endswith(trailer)

def test_format_trailer_commit_without_begin_run():
    """A trailer with no recorded command still has a subject."""
    end = datetime.datetime(2026, 1, 1, 10, 0, 5)
    trailer = format_run_trailer("", None, end, 0)
    message = format_trailer_commit(
        None, [], "abc123", trailer,
    )
    assert message.startswith("Run-End: (unknown) (none)")
@


\section{Full lifecycle test}

To verify the complete workflow, we simulate two runs: one on unchanged
code and one after an edit.
Both produce a header and a trailer commit, and only the second changes
the tree:

<<test functions>>=
def test_full_lifecycle(workdir):
    """Simulate two runs: one with changes, one without."""
    repo = LearnlogRepo(workdir)

    # First run, on the code as initially committed.
    t1 = datetime.datetime(2026, 1, 1, 10, 0)
    repo.begin_run("hello.py", [], t1)
    repo.finalize_run(
        io_log="stdout: hello\n",
        exception_info=None,
        end_time=datetime.datetime(2026, 1, 1, 10, 0, 5),
        exit_code=0,
    )

    # Edit file
    (workdir / "hello.py").write_text('print("v2")\n')

    # Second run, on the edited code.
    t2 = datetime.datetime(2026, 1, 1, 10, 5)
    repo.begin_run("hello.py", [], t2)
    repo.finalize_run(
        io_log="stdout: v2\n",
        exception_info=None,
        end_time=datetime.datetime(2026, 1, 1, 10, 5, 2),
        exit_code=0,
    )

    subjects = repo.git(
        "log", "--reverse", "--format=%s"
    ).stdout.strip().split("\n")
    assert subjects == [
        "learnlog: initial commit",
        "Run: hello.py (none)",
        "Run-End: hello.py (none)",
        "Run: hello.py (none)",
        "Run-End: hello.py (none)",
    ]
    assert repo.git(
        "show", "HEAD:hello.py"
    ).stdout == 'print("v2")\n'
@
